592186045417 true] [17592186115073 1071 "Find the inverse of the elementary matrix \n![](images/03/03d58fbe64af980e170a695b5f511df7e14cd74cc894e802ce5d781642d58b2b.svg)" 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186115073 1071 "Find the inverse of the elementary matrix \n![](images/65/65e27c97ac04ff5514c6e1be3b010e7a6efa464131bfbad03a569f85e728ac85.png)" 13194139534801 17592186045417 false] [17592186115073 1074 "We write down $E$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[E\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{5} & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:=\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore, \n![](images/7b/7bb507644e3602983ed613dbe3b630fbd4fe72799c5412272a072a39c736cbc5.svg)" 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186115073 1074 "We write down $E$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[E\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{5} & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:=\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore, \n![](images/cd/cd8839007e548fcd0858ebc42904d3659d639da834b8caa476c4765b5b1a6b69.png)" 13194139534801 17592186045417 false] [17592186115074 1071 "Find the product $E_1 E_{2}^{-1},$ where ![](images/ad/ad2295d047cfcd60a05c2b155e8c21c511d295e76b0ad4056cf136fe54c272a1.png) and ![](images/29/299ce95f91a5eb4029b2a8512c0b45c24562fac91cc4c70a7e00ecf42c320099.png)" 13194139534801 17592186045417 false] [17592186115074 1071 "Find the product $E_1 E_{2}^{-1},$ where ![](images/c2/c26c31fb0c03ed31508d71c394a0cac97706b8e98f495758ef1bc3cb7b9de37f.svg) and ![](images/27/2728c629af340140b991c75f58104833304f4dc82d6f2f04b29b6ddde363b349.svg)" 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186115074 1074 "Notice that $E_1$ and $E_2$ are both elementary matrices.\n\nFirst, we need to find $E_2^{-1}.$ We write down $E_2$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[{E}_{2}\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n![](images/11/11f12b427f63eb036d2f19210ab85c0a5b279bfda547031e8ae867d130aafc15.svg)\n\nNow, we can find the product $E_1 E_{2}^{-1}\\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/4c/4c46704dbedb6bb10fd40107493f49481923b4f1e524dc8f3f84d98aa4f66256.svg)" 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186115074 1074 "Notice that $E_1$ and $E_2$ are both elementary matrices.\n\nFirst, we need to find $E_2^{-1}.$ We write down $E_2$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[{E}_{2}\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n![](images/a8/a8c0d76a5f06d1077eb45b6af4761434164602747f54d9644646b654d319b373.png)\n\nNow, we can find the product $E_1 E_{2}^{-1}\\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/f1/f16a0b0e573cf3a8022f9c62993364a0d708c3a0cff0b4bb4cb0eadde7848f56.png)" 13194139534801 17592186045417 false] [17592186115243 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436527 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186115243 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436529 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186115244 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436525 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186115244 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436526 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186115244 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436528 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186115245 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436530 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186115245 1038 #edb/ref 17592186436531 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186115472 1050 480.0 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186115735 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186115735 1071 "Find the product $E_{1}E_{2}^{-1}$, where\n$E_{1}|= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, E_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139534801 17592186045417 false] [17592186115735 1071 "Find the product ${E}_{1}{E}_{2}^{-1}$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{E}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186115735 1074 "Notice that ${E}_{1}$ and ${E}_{2}$ are both elementary matrices.\n\nFirst, we need to find ${E}_{2}^{-1}.$ We write down ${E}_{2}$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[{E}_{2}\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{E}_{2}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the product ${E}_{1}{E}_{2}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n{E}_{1}{E}_{2}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-11 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186115839 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186115839 1071 "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,S=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186115839 1071 "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$P = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, Q = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, S = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139534801 17592186045417 false] [17592186115839 1074 "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrix $P$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =P\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrices $Q$ and $S$ are not elementary matrices because they can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\nTherefore, only $P$ is an elementary matrix." 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186115842 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186115842 1071 "Find the inverse of the elementary matrix\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139534801 17592186045417 false] [17592186115842 1071 "Find the inverse of the elementary matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 1\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186115842 1074 "We write down $A$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+7{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 7 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186116495 1064 "An **elementary matrix** is a matrix that can be obtained from the identity matrix by applying *only one* elementary row operation.\n\nFor example, starting with the $2 \\times 2$ identity matrix ![](images/09/09dfa5716dbd500a4684f62a5c409a392761e7546c7ac5e4b592133623552506.svg) we can apply a single elementary row operation to get any of the matrices below:\n\n![](images/44/44bf82527f952ba4d01bbc16f0b81a47fe9e82f447a996f75bccef5171d92242.svg)\n\n- $E_1$ is obtained from $I_2$ by applying the row operation $R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2.$\n\n- $E_2$ is obtained from $I_2$ by applying the row operation $R_1:=2R_1.$\n\n- $E_3$ is obtained from $I_2$ by applying the row operation $R_2 := R_2 + (-7)R_1.$" 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186116495 1064 "An **elementary matrix** is a matrix that can be obtained from the identity matrix by applying *only one* elementary row operation.\n\nFor example, starting with the $2 \\times 2$ identity matrix $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ we can apply a single elementary row operation to get any of the matrices below:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $E_1$ is obtained from $I_2$ by applying the row operation $R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2.$\n\n- $E_2$ is obtained from $I_2$ by applying the row operation $R_1:=2R_1.$\n\n- $E_3$ is obtained from $I_2$ by applying the row operation $R_2 := R_2 + (-7)R_1.$" 13194139534801 17592186045417 false] [17592186116496 1064 "Each elementary matrix $E$ is invertible, and the corresponding inverse is always an elementary matrix of the same type as $E$.\nTo illustrate, let's consider the matrix \n\n![](images/01/0144b651cc6eff46265156e1491f58aacfc167da6dce571dbf4a92c9d94e7177.svg)\nTo find the inverse of $E,$ we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1:** Write down $\\color{blue}E$ and the identity matrix $I_2$ side-by-side, as shown below:\n![](images/fb/fbb291c4f2d7ccc79939cf9368579b07f6649709f123fb70a1383deefaec6fc1.svg)\n\n**Step 2:** Apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix. In our case, we need to apply the transformation $R_2 := R_2 + (-4)R_1.$ We apply the operation to the entire row (not only the left part):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[E\\,|\\,{I}_{2}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[{I}_{2}\\,|\\,{E}^{-1}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix $E^{-1}$ is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore,\n![](images/1e/1e71a52dda452f98fa77c01117f40d055fa0661100a0e4ffc25147d9baefc0af.svg)\n\nWe can work out the inverse of all elementary $2 \\times 2$ matrices in a similar way." 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186116496 1064 "Each elementary matrix $E$ is invertible, and the corresponding inverse is always an elementary matrix of the same type as $E$.\nTo illustrate, let's consider the matrix \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo find the inverse of $E,$ we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1:** Write down $\\color{blue}E$ and the identity matrix $I_2$ side-by-side, as shown below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix. In our case, we need to apply the transformation $R_2 := R_2 + (-4)R_1.$ We apply the operation to the entire row (not only the left part):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[E\\,|\\,{I}_{2}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[{I}_{2}\\,|\\,{E}^{-1}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that [math]R_2 := R_2 + (-4)R_1[/math] transforms [math]E[/math] back into the identity matrix. Likewise, when this row operation is applied to the identity matrix, it generates the inverse of [math]E^{-1}.[/math]\n\nThe matrix $E^{-1}$ is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe can work out the inverse of all elementary $2 \\times 2$ matrices in a similar way." 13194139534801 17592186045417 false] [17592186436473 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]" 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186436473 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186436473 1083 #uuid "03632a2d-f2ff-5829-baea-8d2ea743e840" 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186436474 1080 "None" 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186436474 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186436474 1083 #uuid "081decf6-8b8b-5b4e-bc55-884af37deef0" 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186436475 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186436475 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186436475 1083 #uuid "0d928832-0507-5fdf-88b2-89972940e5e3" 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186436476 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 25\\end{aligned}]" 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186436476 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186436476 1083 #uuid "0dd0fc74-bae7-5ca6-8b3d-6e3dc367d94c" 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186436477 1080 "[\\be