"q-130800" 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186436526 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186436526 1071 "Find the inverse of the elementary matrix \n$W=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186436526 1072 #uuid "682600e4-1f6c-58ba-ae45-caa8f795950e" 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186436526 1074 "We write down $V$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[W\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{6} & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:=6{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{W}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186436527 1068 #edb/ref 17592186436520 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186436527 1069 "q-31889" 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186436527 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186436527 1071 "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,E=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,F=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186436527 1072 #uuid "73b54284-6d5f-5b76-a388-21fc0778bb8a" 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186436527 1074 "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrix $D$ is not an elementary matrix because it can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\n- The matrix $E$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $F$ is not an elementary matrix because it can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\nTherefore, only $E$ is an elementary matrix." 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186436528 1068 #edb/ref 17592186436518 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186436528 1069 "q-73643" 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186436528 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186436528 1071 "Find the inverse of the elementary matrix \n$Q=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186436528 1072 #uuid "9e42534d-d33e-5203-bcb9-d3b1ae0266de" 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186436528 1074 "We write down $Q$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[Q\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{Q}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186436529 1068 #edb/ref 17592186436519 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186436529 1069 "q-42704" 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186436529 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186436529 1071 "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,N=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186436529 1072 #uuid "bb398bcb-fa77-5232-9d5f-318cb8cb3320" 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186436529 1074 "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrices $M$, $N$ and $P$ are not elementary matrices because they can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation." 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186436530 1068 #edb/ref 17592186436523 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186436530 1069 "q-130825" 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186436530 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186436530 1071 "Find the product ${E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{E}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186436530 1072 #uuid "c2f3128b-e14c-5d17-b8ee-71ffa4c47e70" 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186436530 1074 "Notice that ${E}_{1}$ and ${E}_{2}$ are both elementary matrices.\n\nFirst, we need to find ${E}_{1}^{-1}.$ We write down ${E}_{1}$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[{E}_{1}\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{E}_{1}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the product ${E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n{E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186436531 1068 #edb/ref 17592186436521 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186436531 1069 "q-73648" 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186436531 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186436531 1071 "Find the product ${E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{E}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186436531 1072 #uuid "cf425079-a50e-5390-afb0-f23f208d7e4f" 13194139534801 17592186045417 true] [17592186436531 1074 "Notice that ${E}_{1}$ and ${E}_{2}$ are both elementary matrices.\n\nFirst, we need to find ${E}_{1}^{-1}.$ We write down ${E}_{1}$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[{E}_{1}\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}^{-1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the product ${E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n{E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 25\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139534801 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-03632a2d-f2ff-5829-baea-8d2ea743e840" 17592186436473 "answer-081decf6-8b8b-5b4e-bc55-884af37deef0" 17592186436474 "answer-0d928832-0507-5fdf-88b2-89972940e5e3" 17592186436475 "answer-0dd0fc74-bae7-5ca6-8b3d-6e3dc367d94c" 17592186436476 "answer-111040c6